<main><article class="editorial-story"><header class="story-hero"><p class="section-label">Paris–Roubaix · 1899</p><h1>From cobbles<br/><em>to spark plugs.</em></h1><p>Albert Champion won Paris–Roubaix at twenty. Ten years later he was in Flint making the small engine part that would make his initials better known than his racing results.</p></header>
<figure class="story-image hero portrait"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Young Albert Champion leaning forward on a track bicycle" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>Albert Champion as a young rider in the 1890s. From Bocquillon's collection <em>Les vieilles gloires du cycle</em>; public domain.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="story-copy"><p class="lead">On 2 April 1899, Albert Champion rode alone towards Roubaix. A motor pacer was ahead of him. Several of the finest road riders of the day were behind.</p>
<h2>A track rider on the road</h2><p>The fourth Paris–Roubaix covered 268 kilometres. Small motorcycles and motor tricycles paced the race, bringing track specialists onto the road. Champion understood speed and shelter from the velodrome, but the cobbles were another matter.</p><p>He attacked early. Émile Bouhours came close before his pacer struck a spectator. Champion carried on and reached the Roubaix velodrome in 8 hours 22 minutes 53 seconds. He was still twenty; his twenty-first birthday came three days later.</p><p>The victory earned him the place in the race's history now marked by a stone in Roubaix. Cycling, however, proved to be only the first part of his story.</p>
<figure class="story-image inset"><OptimizedImage src={portrait} alt="Albert Champion in racing clothes leaning over the handlebar of his bicycle" widths={[440,720]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 720px"/><figcaption>Champion raced both on the road and in motor-paced events on the track. Historical photograph, public domain.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>From pedals to engines</h2><p>Champion travelled between France and the United States and continued racing bicycles, motorcycles and cars. A later story says the move also helped him avoid French military service. The motive is difficult to establish; his growing interest in engine ignition is not.</p><p>In Boston in 1905, he founded the Albert Champion Company with investors Frank and Spencer Stranahan. The business imported French electrical equipment and soon produced porcelain-insulated spark plugs bearing the Champion name.</p><p>The partnership did not last. Champion left while his partners retained the rights to the name. The <em>Champion</em> brand therefore continued without the man named Champion.</p>
<h2>Two brands from one name</h2><p>In 1908 Champion showed his plugs to William C. Durant of Buick. Durant helped establish a workshop in Flint, Michigan, called Champion Ignition Company. It supplied Buick, but the old Boston firm still controlled the Champion trademark.</p><p>The dispute was settled in 1922. The Flint business became <strong>AC Spark Plug Company</strong>: A and C for Albert Champion. It became a General Motors division in 1927. One French racing cyclist had thus helped give rise to two American spark-plug businesses.</p><p>AC plugs were also used in Charles Lindbergh's <em>Spirit of St. Louis</em> on the 1927 Atlantic crossing. Champion had moved from paced cycle racing to an industry in which reliable ignition was the whole point.</p>
<h2>The final visit to Paris</h2><p>Albert Champion died in Paris on 26 October 1927, aged 49. The contemporary report in <em>Time</em> gave no cause of death. Later biographies linked his collapse to a violent scene of jealousy followed by a heart attack. That is how the story has often been remembered, though its details are less secure than the race result and company history.</p><p>General Motors acquired the remaining interests in AC after his death. Its name survives within ACDelco. The stone in Roubaix says only Albert Champion and 1899. That is enough for the cycling part. The rest of his name ended up under the bonnet.</p></div>
<figure class="story-image closing"><OptimizedImage src={pave} alt="Cobblestone in Roubaix bearing the name Albert Champion and the year 1899" widths={[480,768,960]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 760px"/><figcaption>Albert Champion's winner's stone in Roubaix marks the 1899 victory. Image supplied by the user; public domain.</figcaption></figure>
